  "summary": "Three colors per triangle and not one more facet than needed.",
  "description": "Low poly began as hardware reality — PlayStation and Nintendo 64 budgets meant characters of a few hundred triangles — and became an aesthetic when Unity-era indie artists discovered that few polygons with flat shading, strong silhouettes and pastel gradient lighting reads as calm, modern and affordable. Platforms (Sketchfab, Itch.io) and mobile games standardized the look: faceted terrain, cone-trees, capsule people, isometric game boards. Motion designers (Gorilla, studio reels) adopted the style for explainer geometry; the low-poly landscape render became a stock-image genre of its own.",
